  "answer_text": "In biology, the **cytoskeleton** is a complex, dynamic network of interlinking protein filaments and tubules located within the cytoplasm of a cell. \n\nJust as our bony skeleton provides structure and support to our bodies, the cytoskeleton does the same for cells. However, unlike our rigid bones, the cytoskeleton is highly dynamic; it can rapidly disassemble and reassemble to allow cells to change shape, divide, and move.\n\nThe cytoskeleton is found in all eukaryotic cells (plants, animals, fungi, and protists). While once thought to be unique to eukaryotes, scientists have discovered that prokaryotic cells (bacteria and archaea) also have a simplified cytoskeleton.\n\n---\n\n### The Three Main Components (in Eukaryotes)\nEukaryotic cytoskeletons are made up of three primary types of protein fibers, distinguished by their size, composition, and specific functions:\n\n#### 1. Microfilaments (Actin Filaments)\n*   **Structure:** The thinnest of the fibers (about 7 nanometers in diameter). They are composed of two intertwined strands of a globular protein called **actin**.\n*   **Functions:** \n    *   **Tension-bearing:** They help the cell withstand pulling forces, maintaining its shape.\n    *   **Cell movement:** Working alongside a motor protein called **myosin**, microfilaments enable muscle contraction and amoeboid movement (crawling).\n    *   **Cell division:** They form a \"contractile ring\" during cytokinesis to pinch one cell into two.\n\n#### 2. Intermediate Filaments\n*   **Structure:** Medium-sized fibers (8 to 12 nanometers in diameter) made of various fibrous proteins, most notably **keratin** (the same protein found in your hair and nails). They are more permanent and stable than the other two types of filaments.\n*   **Functions:**\n    *   **Structural support:** They are incredibly strong and designed to resist mechanical stress, preventing the cell from stretching too much.\n    *   **Anchoring organelles:** They hold the nucleus and other organelles securely in place within the cell.\n\n#### 3. Microtubules\n*   **Structure:** The thickest of the fibers (about 25 nanometers in diameter). They are hollow tubes made of a protein called **tubulin** (alpha and beta-tubulin dimers). Like microfilaments, they are highly dynamic and can quickly grow or shrink.\n*   **Functions:**\n    *   **Intracellular transport:** They act as \"railroad tracks\" through the cell. Motor proteins (like kinesin and dynein) carry vesicles, proteins, and organelles along these tracks.\n    *   **Cell division:** During mitosis, microtubules form the **spindle fibers** that latch onto chromosomes and pull them apart.\n    *   **Motility structures:** They make up the internal structural core of **cilia** and **flagella**, the whip-like appendages cells use to swim or sweep away fluids.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Key Functions\nOverall, the cytoskeleton is essential for several critical cellular processes:\n1.  **Maintaining Cell Shape:** It prevents the cell membrane from collapsing and maintains its 3D structure.\n2.  **Cell Motility:** It allows cells to crawl (like white blood cells chasing bacteria) or swim (like sperm cells).\n3.  **Internal Organization:** It positions organelles inside the cell and directs the movement of molecules to their proper destinations.\n4.  **Mitosis (Cell Division):** It physically organizes and separates genetic material so that daughter cells get exact copies of DNA.",
